<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  App Transport Security exemption for the webview — the file's other job
	  is the background-audio declaration further down.

	  A self-hosted la musica server is plain http on an address only the user
	  knows: a LAN IP, a Tailscale/MagicDNS name, or 127.0.0.1:8000 for the
	  Docker container running on the same machine. TLS is not optional for the
	  project to run without it - the server ships without a certificate, so
	  every request the React UI makes (fetch, WebSocket, audio/video playback)
	  is http:// or ws://. ATS blocks exactly those requests by default, which
	  is what makes the app unusable against a real install.

	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent keeps the exemption inside the webview:
	  the shell's native URLSession calls (there are none today - lib.rs has no
	  HTTP client) stay under full ATS, and NSExceptionDomains cannot be used
	  here because the host is chosen by the user at runtime, not by us.

	  Tauri merges this file into the generated plist for both iOS
	  (`tauri ios build`, which is told about it explicitly through
	  `bundle.iOS.infoPlist` in tauri.conf.json) and the macOS .app bundle,
	  so one file covers both Apple platforms. Android is unaffected and
	  documented in desktop/README.md.
	-->
	<!--
	  Audio keeps playing when the app is not in front — a music client that
	  stopped the moment the screen locked would be useless.

	  This is the mode the app can claim honestly, because it IS a music
	  player. It used to carry a second job as well: keeping the process (and
	  so the embedded backend) alive in the background with a silent playback
	  session. The app hosts no backend any more — the server is the user's own
	  Docker container — so nothing here holds a session open for its own sake,
	  which also removes what Apple's review guidance names as abuse.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>

	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
		<true/>
		<!--
		  The blanket key as well, and the reason is MEDIA. `…InWebContent`
		  covers what the web content process fetches — the page, its scripts,
		  its XHRs — but an <audio>/<video> element's bytes are loaded by the
		  media stack, which WebKit runs outside that process on modern iOS, and
		  ATS decisions there read this key. With only the web-content key set,
		  a phone could load the whole app and then fail every single track with
		  no error the page can see: "pressing play just pauses it immediately"
		  is the shape that report takes. This build is a sideloaded client for
		  a server the user runs themselves over plain http on an address only
		  they know; there is no certificate to validate and no App Store review
		  to answer to.
		-->
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
		<!--
		  iOS 14+ asks the user before an app may talk to devices on the local
		  network, and the prompt only exists if the app declares why it wants
		  to. Without this key the permission cannot even be requested, so a
		  server on the LAN (the normal case) is unreachable — while a Tailscale
		  address, which is not "local network" traffic, still works, which is
		  what makes the failure look like a bug in the app rather than a
		  missing prompt.
		-->
		<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
		<string>la musica connects to the la musica server you run on your own network — on this device, on your LAN, or through a VPN.</string>
	</dict>

	<!--
	  The name under the home-screen icon. Without it the generated plist has
	  only CFBundleName, and iOS falls back to that - which today happens to be
	  "la musica" because the CLI maps PRODUCT_NAME from productName by
	  template. Both keys are named here so the sideloaded IPA shows the
	  product's own name rather than whatever the template does with it.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>la musica</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>la musica</string>

	<!--
	  Export-compliance declaration for the App Store question every upload
	  asks. `false` is the honest answer for this app: it implements no
	  cryptography of its own - HTTPS, the keychain and the TLS stack are the
	  system's, and a self-hosted server is reached over plain http. Apple
	  accepts this key in place of answering the question per build, which
	  matters for the sideload flow (an ad-hoc re-sign of our unsigned IPA)
	  because a re-signed bundle cannot be edited to add it afterwards without
	  breaking the signature.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	  Orientation, iPhone and iPad separately because iOS reads the `~ipad`
	  suffix. iPhone omits upside-down, the usual choice: nothing in the UI is
	  drawn for it (the shell rotates fine, the player bar and library just
	  ride along) and iOS only rotates to upside-down when an app asks. iPad
	  gets all four - the library and the tag editors have the room for either
	  landscape. Declared here instead of left to tauri-cli's project.yml
	  template so a reader of this repo knows what the app ships with.
	-->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
